Anna Garcia a seamstress and rural  Bolivia is aiming to make it big with  traditional Bolivian folk music ever  since Evo Morales became the country's  first indigenous president traditional  music has been growing in popularity  will Anna Garcia finally be able to earn  a living with her music it's market day  and by el mozo a suburb of Cochabamba  short  Anna Garcia is a genuine cholita as they  are called here an indigenous woman from  the Bolivian countryside here at the  market she sells skirts and blouses that  she sews herself are already sold  I make them to measure the poeta is a  skirt worn by rural women for Anna  Garcia it's part of the Bolivian  identity more than 30 euros apiece  the garments are not cheap Anna Garcia  has worn a poeta since she was a child  okay Callie and I always work on Yoona  they're so warm I feel naked when I  don't have one on oh yeah makes me  strong I can lift heavier weights  because it fits snugly around my hips no  matter what the load we can carry it it  makes me so strong I could beat him up I  stop a little bigger the skirt is tied  so tightly that it really does help you  carry heavy loads Anna's husband France  is familiar with his wife's love of the  skirts he's been married to her for 35  years  next day they head out to shoot Ana  Garcia's new DVD she's hoping to reach a  larger audience with folk dances like  the quake this is how the cueca goes we  asked a partner to dance first we tapped  like this that's how we did Olivia she's  pinning her hopes of a breakthrough on  this music video the Garcias planned to  post it on YouTube and Facebook and a  son Franklin is directing just a little  bit forward right two steps a little  closer Annabelle's wearing her high  heels mama I love this way the rest of  the family have joined in to support her  the daughter's dance  and husband Frances on the guitar mows  the scattered trying to preserve the  culture of the cholita with our videos  my mother Anna wants this musical  tradition later in the day in the center  of Cochabamba it's time to advance her  career Anna Garcia wants to do more than  perform at parties she wants to be on TV  which is why she has approached Don  Laurel a well-known music producer  he created the showed that cosmos a TV  talent show for traditional indigenous  Bolivian music it's very original and  down-to-earth she breathes new life into  these traditional songs with lots of  energy and enthusiasm but for now Anna  will have to wait Don Lauro can't  promise her an appearance on its show  just yet  a day later she's on her way to a sound  studio to work on her new album she  always has her songbook with her all the  lyrics are in Quechua the most spoken  indigenous language in Bolivia caparison  you're Santa  yeah I'm singing about st. James this is  a folk song in Quechua I write my songs  myself it's about believing  about friendship and love the market for  this kind of music has developed in  Bolivia in recent years and ana Garcia  recognized an opportunity for herself  Ana Garcia's music is rooted in the  Bolivian countryside she grew up here in  Pokhara grande an hour's drive from  Cochabamba there used to be nothing here  but fields this is where she lived as a  child she hasn't been here for 35 years  she's looking for the family home she  still remembers one of the neighbors yes  over there the empty old mud-walled  building was home to Ana and her family  this village is where she first  performed and dreamed of a career as a  musician  her parents were farmers  miss Pappas miss Pappas day me my father  was a drunk my parents used to go out  and leave me alone with the animals and  my brothers and sisters I really  suffered here that's something I've  never forgot the worst moment was when  her mother forbade her to play music  then at the age of 25 France who became  her husband rescued her from her  miserable situation she broke off all  contact to her parents darling  story being Gus I'm doing well thanks to  my talent when I sing and dance I leave  everything behind me  today they have something to celebrate  Anna Garcia is finally going to be on  television  Don Lauro has invited her to appear in  his show the entire family is here all  the erasers in anytime  none of Anna's children dressed in  traditional bolivian garb on a daily  basis even though she tried her best to  convince them to do so I always wanted  to dress my daughters in the foyer but I  think they were a little bit embarrassed  in front of their friends very practical  boy Jana is very heavy  you can't move very freely in it now the  big day has arrived there showed the  cosmos with Don Lauro  Anna Garcia is excited soon everyone  across Bolivia will see her  everyone is watching today the audience  here and the people in front of their TV  screens at home Anna Garcia she's here  today with some wonderful musicians  they'll perform the song sucker Bellator  panel will sing four songs and hopefully  win over the audience  her son Franklin is recording the big  moment and by the third song the ice has  been broken  Oh  it was great a big success I'm very  happy I love to dance and sing the TV  appearance was a huge step forward in  her music career now Anna Garcia is  hoping to perform at concerts and other  events until one day she gets her big  break
 

